    "vulnerable_behavior": "On sigreturn the kernel tries to restore FPU/xstate directly from the user-supplied sigframe (xrstor_from_user_sigframe). If that user access faults (-EFAULT), restore_fpregs_from_user() enters a retry loop and faults the buffer in with fault_in_readable(buf, size). In the vulnerable code the size handed to fault_in_readable() is derived from userspace: __fpu_restore_sig() sets state_size = fx_sw_user.xstate_size (and on the non-xsave path fpu_kernel_xstate_size) and passes it down as the 'size' parameter. fx_sw_user.xstate_size is fully attacker-controlled (only upper-bounded), so a crafted sigreturn frame can make xstate_size smaller than the memory span XRSTOR actually touches, which is determined by the fx_sw_user.xfeatures bits. If the tail of the buffer (beyond xstate_size but within the xfeatures-required span) is unmapped, fault_in_readable(buf, xstate_size) succeeds, XRSTOR faults again, and the kernel spins forever in the retry loop (local denial of service).",
    "trigger_condition": "sigreturn/rt_sigreturn with an xsave sigframe where fx_sw_user.xstate_size is smaller than the extent required by valid bits in fx_sw_user.xfeatures, with the region between buf+xstate_size and the xfeatures-required end unmapped.",
